16
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,304
that can evolve with the business.

17
00:00:59,178 --> 00:01:02,869
At the same time, we need to preserve
the reliability of the platform

18
00:01:03,256 --> 00:01:05,029
our customers already depend upon.

19
00:01:06,228 --> 00:01:10,145
The word I want you to keep in mind
throughout the session is control.

20
00:01:11,015 --> 00:01:15,232
Control over which requests move to
the new implementation, control over

21
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,366
how quickly we expand that rollout, and
control over when we pause or redirect

22
00:01:20,384 --> 00:01:24,252
traffic if the new implementations
does not meet our expectations.

23
00:01:25,447 --> 00:01:30,382
We often think of feature flags as
switches for application features.

24
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:35,747
Today, we'll explore their roles as a
control layer for architectural migration.

25
00:01:37,564 --> 00:01:41,767
Let's begin with why payment monoliths
become so difficult to change

26
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:45,271
Monoliths that outline their design.

27
00:01:45,741 --> 00:01:48,687
First, I want to acknowledge
something important.

28
00:01:48,719 --> 00:01:52,282
Monoliths were less, not necessarily
the wrong architectural choice.

29
00:01:53,292 --> 00:01:56,092
Enterprise payment platforms
were built around the centralized

30
00:01:56,125 --> 00:01:59,217
control, transaction reliability,
and operational simplicity.

31
00:02:00,166 --> 00:02:03,732
For years, those priorities
served the business well, but

32
00:02:03,794 --> 00:02:05,484
the business continued to evolve.

33
00:02:05,977 --> 00:02:10,475
Digital wallets, new payment rails,
loyalty programs, hosted checkouts,

34
00:02:11,112 --> 00:02:14,732
and regional compliance requirements
added new responsibilities.

35
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:21,220
Over the platform-- Over the time, the
platform became, responsible for far more

36
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:23,502
than its original design anticipated.

37
00:02:24,789 --> 00:02:27,015
The problem is not the
size of the code base.

38
00:02:27,017 --> 00:02:29,954
The problem is the growth of the
coordination required to change it.

39
00:02:31,125 --> 00:02:35,646
A change in one capability may
require understanding shared data,

40
00:02:36,435 --> 00:02:41,382
internal dependencies, and assumptions
over several other capabilities.

41
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:47,683
A full rewrite may look attractive, but
an all or nothing cutover concentrates

42
00:02:48,036 --> 00:02:50,142
risk into one major transition.

43
00:02:51,337 --> 00:02:54,491
Today's discussion is about
a more controlled alternative

44
00:02:55,491 --> 00:02:57,398
So what this session covers.

45
00:02:57,736 --> 00:02:59,868
We'll explore four connected topics.

46
00:03:00,099 --> 00:03:05,278
First, domain-driven design, how
to identify meaningful boundaries

47
00:03:05,363 --> 00:03:06,644
within a payment platform.

48
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:14,024
Second, feature flags as a control
layer, how to control routing between

49
00:03:14,025 --> 00:03:15,695
legacy and new implementation.

50
00:03:17,037 --> 00:03:21,825
Third, migration patterns,
strangler flag, anti-corruption

51
00:03:21,827 --> 00:03:24,783
layer, and branch by extraction.

52
00:03:25,289 --> 00:03:30,820
And fourth, execution discipline,
how to sequence extraction, manage

53
00:03:30,832 --> 00:03:34,442
risk, and prevent flags from
becoming permanent technical debt.

54
00:03:35,474 --> 00:03:39,167
Think of these as parts of one approach.

55
00:03:39,789 --> 00:03:42,934
Domain-driven design tells us
where the boundary should be.

56
00:03:43,392 --> 00:03:46,534
Migration patterns help us
introduce those boundaries.

57
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:51,080
Feature flags help us control
the transition, and operational

58
00:03:51,087 --> 00:03:53,542
discipline helps us complete it

59
00:03:54,542 --> 00:03:57,116
Why payment monoliths are hard to break?

60
00:03:58,088 --> 00:04:00,017
There are three major challenges.

61
00:04:00,266 --> 00:04:02,586
The first is transactional integrity.

62
00:04:03,897 --> 00:04:07,733
The existing system may rely on
shared database transactions to

63
00:04:07,738 --> 00:04:10,094
keep related operational consistent.

64
00:04:11,291 --> 00:04:15,303
When we separate the responsibilities,
we must examine which guarantees we

65
00:04:15,303 --> 00:04:20,616
are changing, not simply move the code
and assume those guarantees follow it.

66
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:25,101
The second challenge is the
implicit domain coupling.

67
00:04:26,327 --> 00:04:30,746
Dispute handling, loyalty, reporting,
and other capabilities may share tables,

68
00:04:30,799 --> 00:04:32,601
business logic, and internal calls.

69
00:04:33,438 --> 00:04:37,198
These dependencies may not appear
clearly in architecture diagrams.

70
00:04:37,606 --> 00:04:41,686
They often become visible only
when we try to extract something.

71
00:04:42,664 --> 00:04:45,414
The third challenge is
operational confidence.

72
00:04:46,094 --> 00:04:49,772
Teams have established alerts,
runbooks, and service level expectations

73
00:04:49,834 --> 00:04:51,498
around one deployable system.

74
00:04:52,506 --> 00:04:55,959
A new service introduces a
different operational boundary.

75
00:04:56,443 --> 00:05:01,616
So extraction is not merely a
code movement exercise, it changes

76
00:05:01,628 --> 00:05:05,763
how we reason about correctness,
ownership, and production support

77
00:05:06,763 --> 00:05:11,914
So now let's see the domain-driven design
for payment pla-- payment platforms.

78
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:16,279
Before asking how to extract
a service, we need to ask what

79
00:05:16,279 --> 00:05:17,484
exactly we are extracting.

80
00:05:17,812 --> 00:05:18,625
A package?

81
00:05:18,796 --> 00:05:20,077
A group of endpoints?

82
00:05:20,265 --> 00:05:21,306
A database table?

83
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:23,787
Those are implementation structures.

84
00:05:24,217 --> 00:05:27,214
They do not necessarily represent
a complete business capability.

85
00:05:28,098 --> 00:05:32,878
Domain-driven design, design helps
us start with the business meaning,

86
00:05:33,488 --> 00:05:37,491
which responsibilities are belong
together, which rules belong to this

87
00:05:37,491 --> 00:05:42,676
domain, and where should this domain's
model should influence other domain.

88
00:05:43,623 --> 00:05:47,354
The message of this chapter is
straightforward: define the boundaries

89
00:05:47,407 --> 00:05:49,195
before you break the system apart

90
00:05:50,195 --> 00:05:53,599
Now let's focus on identifying
bounded context in payments.

91
00:05:54,402 --> 00:05:58,664
A bounded context establishes
a clear scope of domain model.

92
00:05:59,668 --> 00:06:02,662
Within that scope, the terminology,
rules, responsibility should

93
00:06:02,664 --> 00:06:04,292
have a consistent meaning.

94
00:06:05,337 --> 00:06:10,193
In a payment platform, authorization,
settlement, disputes, loyalty, and

95
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:15,198
reporting provides useful starting
points for examining those boundaries.

96
00:06:15,799 --> 00:06:19,976
But naming a context is not
the same as isolating it.

97
00:06:20,941 --> 00:06:26,375
Inside the monolith, these capabilities
may still depend on shared schemas,

98
00:06:26,573 --> 00:06:29,181
shared objects, and in-process calls.

99
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:33,645
We need to understand those dependencies
before deciding how to separate them.

100
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:38,760
The practical question is: Can
this domain own its behavior and

101
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:42,544
data without requiring other domain
to understand its implementation?

102
00:06:43,984 --> 00:06:47,440
Also, a bounded context is
a logical boundary first.

103
00:06:47,907 --> 00:06:52,606
It does not automatically require an
immediate move to a separate deployment

104
00:06:53,606 --> 00:06:56,948
Now let's see, ambiguous
language reveals hidden coupling.

105
00:06:57,693 --> 00:07:01,032
One of the most effective ways
to find hidden coupling is to

106
00:07:01,404 --> 00:07:04,512
examine the words teams use.

107
00:07:04,731 --> 00:07:06,000
Take the word transaction.

108
00:07:06,078 --> 00:07:09,318
In authorization, it may
mean an authorization event.

109
00:07:09,424 --> 00:07:12,111
In settlement, it may
mean a financial record.

110
00:07:12,697 --> 00:07:16,464
In dispute handling, it may identify
the payment being challenged.

111
00:07:17,119 --> 00:07:20,535
In loyalty, it may represent
an event that earns rewards.

112
00:07:21,219 --> 00:07:26,065
The word is the same, but the meaning
is different If we force all those

113
00:07:26,078 --> 00:07:29,908
meanings into one shared model, we
can unintentionally present coupling.

114
00:07:30,926 --> 00:07:34,535
Context mapping helps us make
those differences explicit.

115
00:07:35,392 --> 00:07:38,900
Why owns and who owns each concept?

116
00:07:39,652 --> 00:07:43,256
Which domain supplies
information to another, and what

117
00:07:43,295 --> 00:07:44,918
contract should connect them?

118
00:07:46,343 --> 00:07:49,784
The goal is to eliminate
communication between domains.

119
00:07:50,356 --> 00:07:55,860
It is to replace accidental sharing
the-- with intentional integration

120
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:59,386
Feature flags as the
migration control plan.

121
00:08:00,371 --> 00:08:03,714
Now that we understand the boundaries,
we can discuss the feature flags.

122
00:08:03,977 --> 00:08:06,610
A feature flag does not discover
the right domain boundary.

123
00:08:07,022 --> 00:08:10,255
It operates at a boundary
we have already designed.

124
00:08:10,911 --> 00:08:13,836
At that point, the application
can choose between the legacy

125
00:08:13,839 --> 00:08:16,379
implementation and the new service.

126
00:08:17,172 --> 00:08:21,209
This gives us a way to separate
deployment from the traffic exposure.

127
00:08:21,857 --> 00:08:25,549
We can deploy the new implementations,
validate its readiness, and then

128
00:08:25,551 --> 00:08:27,171
introduce traffic deliberately.

129
00:08:28,182 --> 00:08:30,806
This is what I mean by
migration control plane.

130
00:08:31,819 --> 00:08:36,814
A mechanism for controlling how the
system transitions rather than treating

131
00:08:36,814 --> 00:08:39,036
migration as a single deployment event

132
00:08:40,036 --> 00:08:42,527
Flags enable dual path traffic routing.

133
00:08:43,626 --> 00:08:46,063
There are three important
controls on the slide.

134
00:08:46,704 --> 00:08:48,523
The first is percentage rollout.

135
00:08:49,535 --> 00:08:53,063
We can start with a small portion of
eligible traffic, observe the results,

136
00:08:53,156 --> 00:08:55,210
and increase exposure in stages.

137
00:08:56,443 --> 00:08:58,666
Second is cohort targeting.

138
00:08:59,241 --> 00:09:03,872
We can select specific merchants,
transaction types, or regions instead

139
00:09:03,872 --> 00:09:06,464
of exposing every request immediately.

140
00:09:07,638 --> 00:09:10,662
That helps us define a more
deliberate initial scope.

141
00:09:12,384 --> 00:09:15,087
The third is a kill, kill switch.

142
00:09:15,543 --> 00:09:19,832
If the new implementation behaves
unexpectedly, we can stop routing

143
00:09:20,078 --> 00:09:23,737
new requests to it and redirect
eligible traffic to the legacy path.

144
00:09:24,677 --> 00:09:26,957
But there is an implement
important limitation.

145
00:09:27,977 --> 00:09:33,374
Redirecting traffic does not undo payment
actions that have already completed.

146
00:09:34,523 --> 00:09:39,570
So a routing fallback must be supported
by a clear plan for in-flight requests,

147
00:09:39,984 --> 00:09:41,804
data state, and reconciliation.

148
00:09:43,297 --> 00:09:46,973
The flag is one part of that
plan, not the entire plan.

149
00:09:46,973 --> 00:09:46,983
A

150
00:09:47,983 --> 00:09:51,952
tax-- A tax-- A taxonomy
of flag types in migration.

151
00:09:52,818 --> 00:09:55,221
Not all flags serve the same purpose.

152
00:09:55,932 --> 00:09:59,619
A release toggle determines whether
if the new path is available.

153
00:10:00,266 --> 00:10:03,618
It allows us to deploy code
before exposing it to the traffic.

154
00:10:04,792 --> 00:10:08,321
An experiment toggle supports
comparison between the implementations.

155
00:10:09,402 --> 00:10:13,430
In the shadow mode approach
described here, the legacy

156
00:10:13,452 --> 00:10:18,284
result remains authoritative
and while we compare behavior.

157
00:10:19,281 --> 00:10:24,731
For payment workflows, the comparison
must be designed so that shadow

158
00:10:24,731 --> 00:10:28,918
decision does not accidentally
replicate a real financial action.

159
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:34,256
Now, an ops toggle, which is
an operational toggle, gives us

160
00:10:34,298 --> 00:10:37,481
the operations team a runtime
control during an incident.

161
00:10:38,805 --> 00:10:42,654
And a permission toggle enables
selected capabilities for a

162
00:10:42,664 --> 00:10:45,374
particular merchant group or regions.

163
00:10:46,355 --> 00:10:49,976
The takeaway is to make each
flag's purpose explicit.

164
00:10:50,968 --> 00:10:55,392
A deployment control, a comparison
me-mechanism, and an emergency switch

165
00:10:55,839 --> 00:11:01,457
may look similar in code, but they
require different operating expectations.

166
00:11:02,457 --> 00:11:02,565
Excuse me.

167
00:11:02,954 --> 00:11:04,621
migration pattern taxonomy.

168
00:11:05,388 --> 00:11:10,679
Feature flags are most useful when
they support a clear migration pattern.

169
00:11:12,151 --> 00:11:13,006
We'll look at three.

170
00:11:14,358 --> 00:11:20,525
A strangler, fig pattern, which could help
us replace responsibility incrementally.

171
00:11:21,344 --> 00:11:27,535
An anti-corruption layer protects the
new domain model from legacy contacts.

172
00:11:28,448 --> 00:11:32,279
Branch by abstraction lets us
introduce alternative implemented

173
00:11:32,301 --> 00:11:33,733
behind a stable interface.

174
00:11:34,518 --> 00:11:37,212
These patterns, address
different questions.

175
00:11:38,079 --> 00:11:40,963
Where do we intercept the request?

176
00:11:41,059 --> 00:11:43,037
How do we translate between the models?

177
00:11:43,806 --> 00:11:49,123
And how do we switch implementations
while deployment continues?

178
00:11:50,726 --> 00:11:52,359
Let's start with the strangler flag.

179
00:11:53,359 --> 00:11:59,113
Strangler fig, mm, pattern replaces
the legacy systems gradually.

180
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:00,723
There are three phases.

181
00:12:00,929 --> 00:12:02,061
First, intercept.

182
00:12:03,173 --> 00:12:07,933
We introduce a facade or routing point
around the capability being replaced.

183
00:12:08,871 --> 00:12:09,864
Second, divert.

184
00:12:10,331 --> 00:12:14,022
We route selected traffic to the
new implementation and expand the

185
00:12:14,022 --> 00:12:16,203
traffic as validation progresses.

186
00:12:17,078 --> 00:12:18,255
Third, retire.

187
00:12:19,095 --> 00:12:22,875
Once the transition is complete,
the old path is no longer needed.

188
00:12:23,055 --> 00:12:24,891
We remove the legacy responsibility.

189
00:12:25,502 --> 00:12:28,093
The feature flag controls the diversion.

190
00:12:28,562 --> 00:12:33,917
What makes this valuable is that
the implementation, exposure, and

191
00:12:33,951 --> 00:12:36,332
retirement become separate decisions.

192
00:12:36,872 --> 00:12:40,227
We do not have to deploy the new
service, move all traffic, and delete

193
00:12:40,244 --> 00:12:41,949
the old implementation at the same time.

194
00:12:42,938 --> 00:12:45,528
That separation creates room to learn

195
00:12:46,528 --> 00:12:49,126
Anti-corruption layer and
branch-by abstraction.

196
00:12:49,612 --> 00:12:53,204
An anti-corruption layer protects the
new model from legacy representations.

197
00:12:53,821 --> 00:12:57,789
Imagine that the legacy systems uses
one shared structure for concepts that

198
00:12:57,789 --> 00:13:00,707
the new domain needs to distinguish.

199
00:13:00,953 --> 00:13:05,770
Rather than copying that structure
directly into the service, new service,

200
00:13:05,771 --> 00:13:07,466
we translate it at the boundary.

201
00:13:08,483 --> 00:13:13,335
This allows the new domain to
use its own language while still

202
00:13:13,365 --> 00:13:15,095
integrating with the existing system.

203
00:13:15,624 --> 00:13:18,528
Branch-by abstraction
addresses a different concern.

204
00:13:19,272 --> 00:13:22,267
We introduce an interface around
the component being replaced.

205
00:13:22,721 --> 00:13:26,864
Then we provide two implementations
behind that inter-interface, the legacy

206
00:13:26,865 --> 00:13:28,603
version and the new inter-- new version.

207
00:13:29,347 --> 00:13:31,912
The feature flag selects
the appropriate path.

208
00:13:32,525 --> 00:13:34,717
The shared principle is important.

209
00:13:35,635 --> 00:13:40,811
We must identify the, identify and
introduce the abstraction point before

210
00:13:40,812 --> 00:13:42,839
we can use it to control migration.

211
00:13:44,216 --> 00:13:47,387
Flags enforce the seam,
they do not create it

212
00:13:48,387 --> 00:13:52,309
Sequencing attraction, extraction,
sorry, a practical framework.

213
00:13:53,142 --> 00:13:55,333
Not all domains are ready
to move at the same time.

214
00:13:56,170 --> 00:13:59,580
Once we know how to migrate, we need
to decide whether-- where to begin.

215
00:13:59,854 --> 00:14:03,593
It is tempting to choose the domain
with the greatest business visibility

216
00:14:03,593 --> 00:14:06,920
or the largest modernization
opportunity, but that domain

217
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:08,536
may also carry the greater risk.

218
00:14:09,102 --> 00:14:11,935
The first extraction should
help us prove our method.

219
00:14:12,501 --> 00:14:14,363
Can we define ownership clearly?

220
00:14:14,454 --> 00:14:16,074
Can we operate the new service?

221
00:14:16,141 --> 00:14:17,545
Can we compare behavior?

222
00:14:17,765 --> 00:14:20,680
Can we control rollout
and respond to problems?

223
00:14:21,489 --> 00:14:25,612
We want an early candidate that
allows us to learn while keeping

224
00:14:25,612 --> 00:14:27,185
the consequences manageable.

225
00:14:28,168 --> 00:14:31,924
The first migration is not only about
delivering a service, it is about

226
00:14:32,214 --> 00:14:34,191
building a repeatable capability

227
00:14:35,191 --> 00:14:37,879
Now let's see extraction
readiness scoring.

228
00:14:38,325 --> 00:14:43,595
This slide pro-proposes assessing
readiness before committing to extraction.

229
00:14:44,649 --> 00:14:48,806
The purpose is to make the
decision even evidence-based.

230
00:14:49,348 --> 00:14:53,747
we should understand that the domain
boundary, its dependencies, the

231
00:14:53,748 --> 00:14:57,156
operational expec-expectations,
and the transition plan.

232
00:14:58,310 --> 00:15:03,935
A capability that looks small in code
may still be difficult to extract because

233
00:15:04,010 --> 00:15:06,020
other domains depend heavily on its data.

234
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:11,464
A larger capability may be easier
to separate if its responsibilities

235
00:15:11,467 --> 00:15:12,887
are already well-contained.

236
00:15:13,882 --> 00:15:18,156
The score should support the conversation,
not replace architectural judgment.

237
00:15:18,468 --> 00:15:21,617
The principle is start where
you can afford to learn.

238
00:15:22,064 --> 00:15:25,512
Do not confuse business importance
with extraction readiness.

239
00:15:26,512 --> 00:15:28,180
Recommended extraction sequence.

240
00:15:28,700 --> 00:15:31,911
The sequence on this slide is the
best understood from the bottom

241
00:15:31,914 --> 00:15:36,164
upward, moving from the early learning
opportunities toward the payment core.

242
00:15:37,146 --> 00:15:41,113
Reporting and analytics are
proposed as the first candidate.

243
00:15:41,810 --> 00:15:45,944
Loyalty, and rewards may follow
when their rules and other

244
00:15:46,070 --> 00:15:47,602
ownership are sufficiently clear.

245
00:15:48,389 --> 00:15:52,830
Hosted checkout and dispute handling
come next in this suggested progression.

246
00:15:54,536 --> 00:15:58,944
Authorization and settlements at the
latter end because, of their criticality.

247
00:15:59,445 --> 00:16:01,603
This is a proposed sequence,
not a universal rule.

248
00:16:01,629 --> 00:16:04,931
The right order depends on the
actual platforms, its dependencies,

249
00:16:05,001 --> 00:16:07,405
and the readiness of each domain.

250
00:16:08,121 --> 00:16:13,152
The broader point is to build confidence
progressively, demonstrate that the

251
00:16:13,154 --> 00:16:18,435
migration approach works before applying
it to the most consequential flows

252
00:16:19,435 --> 00:16:24,982
Now let's see the common pitfalls and
organizational friction, where migration

253
00:16:24,984 --> 00:16:26,609
stall and how we-- how to require.

254
00:16:26,666 --> 00:16:30,610
Even with a sound migration
pattern, the program can stall.

255
00:16:31,610 --> 00:16:35,407
Pro-- Some problems come from
technical design, others come from

256
00:16:35,439 --> 00:16:40,049
unclear ownership, team structure,
or incomplete definitions of success.

257
00:16:41,042 --> 00:16:44,353
A service can be independently
deployed, still remain tightly

258
00:16:44,371 --> 00:16:46,779
coupled to several other services.

259
00:16:47,586 --> 00:16:51,048
A flag can reach full rollout
while leaving the legacy code

260
00:16:51,048 --> 00:16:52,430
and dependencies untouched.

261
00:16:53,452 --> 00:16:58,219
So we need to measure more than
the number of services created or

262
00:16:58,247 --> 00:16:59,811
the percentage of traffic moved.

263
00:17:00,539 --> 00:17:05,398
We need to ask whether the migration is
producing clearer ownership and reducing

264
00:17:05,402 --> 00:17:07,831
the coordination required for the change.

265
00:17:08,831 --> 00:17:10,905
Four failure modes to anticipate.

266
00:17:11,491 --> 00:17:13,811
The first failure mode
is false decomposition.

267
00:17:14,691 --> 00:17:19,524
We separately-- we separate deployments
but retain shared data dependencies

268
00:17:19,574 --> 00:17:21,735
and coordinated challenges, changes.

269
00:17:22,953 --> 00:17:26,245
The architecture looks disturbed, but
the responsibilities remain entangled.

270
00:17:27,245 --> 00:17:29,227
The second is over-fragmentation.

271
00:17:29,850 --> 00:17:34,546
We create many small services without
gaining meaningful domain boundaries.

272
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:36,939
The third is flag sprawl.

273
00:17:38,571 --> 00:17:43,034
Migration flags accumulate because
they have no owner, expiration

274
00:17:43,034 --> 00:17:45,031
expectation, or removal plan.

275
00:17:46,043 --> 00:17:48,444
The fourth is organizational misalignment.

276
00:17:48,812 --> 00:17:54,657
We design around business domains, but
delivery still depends on hands-off

277
00:17:54,693 --> 00:17:56,428
between technology layer teams.

278
00:17:57,307 --> 00:18:00,875
The architecture and the ownership
model needs to support each other,

279
00:18:01,071 --> 00:18:05,133
otherwise we can reproduce the
monolith coordination problems in

280
00:18:05,135 --> 00:18:07,651
a more dis-distributed environment.

281
00:18:08,651 --> 00:18:10,975
Flag lifecycle dis-discipline.

282
00:18:11,510 --> 00:18:13,666
A migration flag needs a lifecycle.

283
00:18:14,212 --> 00:18:18,115
At creation, define its owner,
its purpose, its expected

284
00:18:18,115 --> 00:18:19,876
lifetime, and its removal task.

285
00:18:20,483 --> 00:18:24,706
During activation, define how
exposure increases and what conditions

286
00:18:24,738 --> 00:18:28,013
require a pause or traffic rollback.

287
00:18:28,617 --> 00:18:33,635
At retirement, remove the flag and
the obsolete implementations when

288
00:18:33,636 --> 00:18:38,742
it is safe to do so Reaching 100%
rollout is not the finish line.

289
00:18:39,260 --> 00:18:41,064
It means traffic has moved.

290
00:18:41,479 --> 00:18:45,104
It do-- does not necessarily
mean the legacy code, the data

291
00:18:45,106 --> 00:18:48,824
dependencies, or operational
responsibilities have disappeared.

292
00:18:49,318 --> 00:18:53,330
A useful completion question is
what can we now safely delete?

293
00:18:54,282 --> 00:18:59,501
Statelessness detection and flag
intervention-- inventories help

294
00:18:59,634 --> 00:19:03,372
keep that question visible rather
than leaving cleanup to memory.

295
00:19:04,372 --> 00:19:05,411
The key takeaways.

296
00:19:05,912 --> 00:19:08,427
Let me bring the session
together with four key takeaways.

297
00:19:09,002 --> 00:19:14,398
First, feature flags can make traffic
migration incremental and controllable.

298
00:19:15,683 --> 00:19:20,693
They help us reduce reliance on a
single all-or-nothing cutover, but they

299
00:19:20,693 --> 00:19:22,862
do not remove every migration risk.

300
00:19:23,753 --> 00:19:28,712
Second, DDD, domain-driven
design, defines the seams, the--

301
00:19:28,770 --> 00:19:30,765
and flags help enforce them.

302
00:19:31,409 --> 00:19:36,142
We need to understand the boundary
before routing traffic across it.

303
00:19:37,126 --> 00:19:42,975
Third, sequence traction extraction by
readiness, not simply by am-ambition.

304
00:19:43,817 --> 00:19:47,501
Use early migrations to establish
operational confidence and

305
00:19:47,501 --> 00:19:49,631
demonstrate that the approach works.

306
00:19:50,881 --> 00:19:55,153
Fourth, manage flag debt
throughout the migration.

307
00:19:56,502 --> 00:20:00,236
Every temporary control needs
ownership and an exit plan.

308
00:20:00,352 --> 00:20:03,819
The objective is not to create
the greatest number of services,

309
00:20:04,173 --> 00:20:08,799
it is to create a platform with
clear responsibilities and more

310
00:20:08,963 --> 00:20:10,571
manageable path for change.

311
00:20:11,571 --> 00:20:13,467
I'll close with this thought.

312
00:20:13,835 --> 00:20:18,141
Migration does not have to be
one large leap from the old

313
00:20:18,141 --> 00:20:19,655
architecture to the new architecture.

314
00:20:20,362 --> 00:20:24,557
It can be sequence-- It can be
a sequence of deliberate steps.

315
00:20:24,924 --> 00:20:29,355
Define the domain, introduce
the seam, control the traffic,

316
00:20:29,663 --> 00:20:34,171
validate the behavior, and
retire what is no longer needed.

317
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:38,293
That is how feature flags become
more than a release mechanism.

318
00:20:39,181 --> 00:20:42,619
They become an engine for
architectural migration.

319
00:20:43,627 --> 00:20:45,133
Thank you all for your time.

320
00:20:45,728 --> 00:20:47,544
I welcome any questions you may have.

321
00:20:47,731 --> 00:20:51,382
Please feel free to reach out, so that
we can continue the con-conversation

322
00:20:51,402 --> 00:20:52,547
after this session as well.

323
00:20:53,540 --> 00:20:55,531
Hope everyone have a nice day.

324
00:20:55,649 --> 00:20:56,484
Thank you so much.

